From 3b65983e9becf28208c1ec62b07d5b45b1c435ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:45:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 106/106] Aurora: in-between frames for what the game moves
 itself

The in-between frame blends each draw's matrices toward the frame before.
What the game moves another way stepped at 30 FPS or was drawn twice:

- The boat's hull, skinned on the CPU and drawn with the bare view matrix:
  the rigid motion that carries three of its vertices from the frame before
  gives the matrices those vertices were drawn with (vertex_motion). A model
  drawn into a shadow map (orthographic) and into the scene is not two
  copies of itself. The sea's strips, which get a new key every frame, are
  blended from where the camera's motion carries them.
- Fast turns: a bone of Link's sword arm turns 90 degrees and more in a game
  frame. A draw with a key of its own may turn 150 degrees (copies still
  41), and a linear part that turned more than 15 degrees is blended as a
  rotation (slerp) times its scales, so it is not shrunk halfway. A copy the
  same size as the draw, the nearest such and near enough, may turn as far:
  a broken pot's shards, one model at random sizes.
- Particles: the game sends their corners as positions. The host writes the
  particle and its age to BP 0x7E and 0x7D (registers the retail GX never
  uses) before each JPA particle draw; gxcore takes them with the next draw
  (DrawPlan::draw_tag), and the draw is paired with the same particle's and
  its positions blended halfway (blend_positions, a second vertex range the
  in-between frame binds). An emitter's callback that draws the boat's bow
  waves and trail announces its emitter and draw count at BP 0x7C and 0x7B;
  those draws pair by their place and blend vertex by vertex.
- The boat's real shadow: its volume (one box for every real shadow) pairs
  with itself while it keeps its place in the view, and the sea triangles it
  is cast on, a different count as the boat sails, pair by the shadow's
  texture, so the projection is blended every frame.

What blend_draw needs of a draw besides its constants (its key, the hull's
samples, a particle's positions) is captured with the draw (DrawInput,
capture_draw), since the helper thread matches it after its plan is gone;
the helper stages a particle's blended vertices in their own area
(push_interp_vertices, g_interpVertexBuffer), and each job resolves to its
block and its vertices.

DOL_AURORA_FRAME_INTERP_TRACE takes a range (first-last), and the trace
shows the tag and whether the positions were blended. frame_interp_test
covers each case; its new checks fail without the change.
